Running the firmware under QEMU
================================

sun4v-qemu-kit builds firmware for QEMU niagara, an emulated OpenSPARC T1.
Also see REQUIREMENTS.LINUX and REQUIREMENTS.SOLARIS.


Quick start
-----------
    git clone https://github.com/unix0cc/sun4v-qemu-kit
    cd sun4v-qemu-kit
    ./configure                 # optional, see README.configure in the repository
    make all                    # saves to ./output; on Solaris: `gmake`
 or
    make all OUTDIR=$HOME/fw    # saves to $HOME/fw (give it to -L)

This builds the default configuration, legion [TARGET=legion], and ends by
printing the command that starts QEMU with it. No disk is needed; you get the
OpenBoot `ok` prompt:

    qemu-system-sparc64 -M niagara -m 2048 -L ./output -nographic \
        -serial mon:stdio

To boot the Solaris 10 image based on Legion, from the OpenSPARC T1 archive,
add the Legion disk (the simulated disk of Sun's Legion simulator for
OpenSPARC):
http://download.oracle.com/technetwork/systems/opensparc/OpenSPARCT1_Arch.1.5.tar.bz2
The file is S10image/disk.s10hw2 in the unpacked archive (here it is unpacked
to $HOME/T1). Then type `boot disk` at the prompt:

    qemu-system-sparc64 -M niagara -m 2048 -L ./output -nographic \
        -serial mon:stdio \
        -drive if=pflash,file=$HOME/T1/S10image/disk.s10hw2,format=raw

    ok boot disk

Solaris boots to `console login:`.

The WARNING lines and "virtual-console not found." printed at the start are
normal.

To boot the Solaris 10 1/13 DVD image instead up to ramdisk, see README.S10U11.

To keep the set somewhere permanent, install it (this copies the built set, it
does not build):

    ./configure --prefix=$HOME/fw    # default /usr/local, needs root
    make all && make install         # on Solaris: `gmake`

`make install` prints the QEMU command for the installed set. More in
"Installing a built set" below.


Rules
-----
- `-m` must equal MD_MEM (2048 for legion, 4096 for pci; RELEASE-INFO lists the
  value of each set).
- Console: use `-nographic -serial mon:stdio`. For a console log file, replace
  `-serial mon:stdio` with:

      -chardev stdio,id=char0,mux=on,signal=off,logfile=<console.log>,\
logappend=on \
      -serial chardev:char0 -mon chardev=char0,mode=readline


legion (the default)
--------------------
The default configuration: `make all`, same as `make all TARGET=legion`.
Legion is Sun's software simulator for OpenSPARC; this configuration presents
the same machine: virtual devices only (a console and an RTC) and one
simulated disk, available via the QEMU pflash interface (the first if=pflash
drive).
Solaris reaches it through the hsimd driver, which stock Solaris images do
not have.

disk.s10hw2 is a Solaris 10 guest with the hsimd driver. The drive is a RAM
copy of the image: writes never reach the file, every boot starts clean, and
its size is added on top of -m.

Solaris reaches its login prompt on unmodified QEMU 8.2.2 up to MD_MEM=2048.
Above that it needs a QEMU with 256M page size support (see
md/legion/README, which also has the full device list).

The Solaris 10 1/13 DVD image also boots, up to its miniroot: README.S10U11.


pci
---
Build it with `make all TARGET=pci`. It runs on a QEMU with the
experimental vpci patches, which are not in upstream, so it does not run on a
stock one. See md/pci/README.


What QEMU loads from -L (and how to check it)
---------------------------------------------
qemu-system-sparc64 -M niagara loads six files from the -L directory:

    reset.bin  q.bin  openboot.bin  1up-hv.bin  1up-md.bin  nvram1

Point -L at the directory with all six (./output after `make all`,
<dir>/<target> in a release). They must come from one build: do not mix files
of two builds.

To see which files QEMU loaded and from where, add -trace load_file. QEMU looks
in the current directory before -L, so a stray openboot.bin there silently
wins:

    qemu-system-sparc64 -M niagara -m 2048 -L ./output -nographic \
        -serial mon:stdio -trace load_file

QEMU prints one line per file before OpenBoot starts; all six should point
into the -L directory:

    load_file name nvram1 location ./output/nvram1
    ...
    load_file name openboot.bin location ./output/openboot.bin


If something goes wrong
-----------------------
  Symptom                         What to do
  ------------------------------  ----------------------------------------------
  Nothing is printed              The files come from two builds, or one is
                                  missing: see "What QEMU loads from -L" above.
  qemu: fatal: Trap 0x0032 ...    -m is smaller than MD_MEM; make them equal.
  MAXTL (6), before any output
  `boot disk` faults              The drive must be if=pflash (legion).
  Solaris panics in               legion above 2048 MB needs a QEMU with 256M
  unix:startup_vm                 page size support (md/legion/README).


Installing a built set
----------------------
`make install` copies the six files of a built set (it does not build) to
$(prefix)/share/sun4v-qemu-kit/<TARGET>/, default prefix /usr/local; -L is
then that directory. DESTDIR stages the install for a package, TARGET=all
installs every configuration, `make uninstall` removes what was installed.
The build itself writes to OUTDIR, not to the prefix.
